    "q-40121": {
      "dom_id": "step-q40121",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Suppose ${b}_{2}=k{b}_{1}-a$ and ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2}=0.$ If $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and ${b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$, find the value of $k.$ \n\n*Hint: You might wish to take the equation ${b}_{2}=k{b}_{1}-a$ and find the dot product of both sides with ${b}_{1}.$*",
      "worked_solution": "First, we take the equation ${b}_{2}=k{b}_{1}-a$ and find the dot product of both sides with ${b}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & =k{b}_{1}-a \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & ={b}_{1}\\cdot (k{b}_{1}-a) \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & ={b}_{1}\\cdot (k{b}_{1})-({b}_{1}\\cdot a) \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & =k({b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1})-({b}_{1}\\cdot a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the formula for the dot product to compute the values of ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{1}\\cdot a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1} & ={b}_{1}^{2}+{b}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot a & ={b}_{1}{a}_{1}+{b}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1},$ ${b}_{1}\\cdot a,$ and ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & =k({b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1})-({b}_{1}\\cdot a) \\\\ 0 & =k\\cdot 4-2 \\\\ -4k & =-2 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
